  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Militant Jihadism shows early but meaningful reach for a 2019 scholarly book: it has been downloaded 3,330 times through OAPEN, is openly available in hybrid form, and is held in at least one library record. The record is still citation-light, with OpenAlex showing 0 citations, so the clearest evidence here is use and accessibility rather than formal scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Militant Jihadism, published by Leuven University Press in 2019 by Şerafettin Pektaş and Johan Leman, addresses a policy-relevant and socially significant topic: the changing landscape of jihadism, especially in relation to Europe and the aftermath of territorial losses by the Islamic State. The work’s subject profile aligns most strongly with jihadism and militant political violence, and its open-access availability gives it a wider potential audience than a closed monograph would have.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is readership. OAPEN reports 3,330 downloads, which indicates sustained engagement with the book beyond initial publication. The monthly download pattern suggests recurring use rather than a one-time burst, pointing to continued interest in the book’s analysis of militant jihadist trajectories and organization. Because downloads are a usage measure rather than a citation metric, they should be read as evidence of access and reading, not as scholarly endorsement.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book’s accessibility is reinforced by its open status in repository records, with open instances available through the publisher and OAPEN. That openness likely supports circulation among researchers, students, and practitioners working on terrorism, religious fundamentalism, Islam, sociology, and related policy fields. Library holdings also show subject classification in areas closely connected to the book’s theme, which is consistent with its use as a reference or teaching resource.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record remains sparse: OpenAlex shows 0 citations. That means the evidence base for scholarly uptake is still thin, and it is best to describe the book’s impact as emerging and usage-centered rather than citation-established. The available record does not show public-event coverage, so there is no basis here for claims about broad media attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are also signs of institutional support and relevance. The funding record notes KU Leuven support, and the book has a recorded connection to the Gender equality SDG in the available metadata, suggesting that the work is being treated as part of a wider policy and social-research conversation. Overall, the record portrays a book with clear topical importance, open distribution, and steady readership, even though formal citation uptake has not yet accumulated.</p>",
